---
/**
 * foodcraft / LocationBN — "Panday Coffee Works" (BN), the LOCATION section.
 *
 * ROLE: where the roastery physically is, and when its door is open. The design
 * draws the spine's rail ("08 / Location") and then splits the content cell into
 * two EQUAL halves with NO gap: on the LEFT, behind a 1px hairline, the address
 * as the headline, an arrival note, a ruled stack of opening-hours rows and a
 * closing mono line; on the RIGHT, full-bleed to the sheet's edge, the map. The
 * division between the halves is a BORDER, never a gutter — that is the whole
 * template — and nothing in this section has a corner.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * element carrying data-component="LOCATION" (id="visit") in
 * "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html":
 *   section    border-bottom 1px #17181A · grid minmax(0,52px) minmax(0,1fr)
 *              → the wrapper's .sec, whose --rail-w IS that 52px
 *   rail       border-right 1px #C8C7C2 · flex end/centre · padding 20px 0 ·
 *              label vertical-rl + rotate(180deg) · mono 10px/.3em/#A8351B
 *              → the wrapper's .rail + .rail-t, used and NOT rebuilt
 *   split      grid minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr), no gap
 *   info cell  padding 48px 36px · border-right 1px #C8C7C2
 *   headline   800 clamp(24px,3vw,40px) / 1.04 / -.03em / uppercase /
 *              margin 0 0 .3em / #17181A / no max-width
 *   sub        15px / 1.78 / #3C3E42 / margin 0 0 24px / max-width 40ch
 *   hours box  border-top 1px #17181A  (FULL strength, not the grey hairline)
 *   hours row  flex · space-between · gap 16px · padding 12px 0 ·
 *              border-bottom 1px #C8C7C2
 *   day cell   mono 10.5px / .12em / uppercase / #5A5C60
 *   time cell  600 14.5px #17181A
 *   note       mono 10px / .1em / uppercase / #5A5C60 / margin 16px 0 0 / 1.9
 *   map cell   position relative · min-height 460px · ground #E4E2DC ·
 *              overflow hidden
 *   plot       repeating-linear-gradient 0deg + 90deg, rgba(23,24,26,.09),
 *              1px line every 40px
 *   roads      top 48% h:5px · left 44% w:4px · rgba(168,53,27,.35)
 *   pin        top 48% left 44% translate(-50%,-100%), centred
 *   dot        13px #A8351B ringed 3px #EFEEEA — SQUARE, like everything here
 *   flag       8px under the dot · mono 9px/.14em caps · #17181A / #EFEEEA ·
 *              padding 5px 10px
 *   caption    left 16px bottom 16px · mono 10px/.16em caps · #5A5C60
 * The map stays on the RIGHT, the split keeps both tracks above 900px, and no
 * division has been converted from a border into a gap. That last habit is what
 * would turn this template back into the soft one it deliberately is not.
 *
 * CLASS NAMES ARE THIS TEMPLATE'S OWN. An audit had to rename a whole set of
 * classes a previous template copied verbatim from a sibling, so nothing here
 * reuses LocationBM's `.tah-visit-*`: every scoped class is prefixed `.pcw-vis-`
 * for Panday Coffee Works. Only the HOUSE STYLE crossed over from LocationBM —
 * this doc block's shape, the tolerant reads, the three-tier map, and the
 * guard-outside-the-tag discipline.
 *
 * R1 · NO LITERAL HEX. Every hex the design writes in this element already has a
 * name in PageBN and is reached only through it:
 *   #17181A --ink (and --rule-strong, see below) · #3C3E42 --ink-2
 *   #5A5C60 --muted · #C8C7C2 --line · #E4E2DC --tint · #A8351B --accent
 *   #EFEEEA --paper (the pin's ring) and --on-ink (the flag's label)
 *   rgba(23,24,26,.09) → rgba(var(--ink-rgb),.09)
 *   rgba(168,53,27,.35) → rgba(var(--accent-rgb),.35)
 * No colour in this element lacked a token; nothing was invented.
 *
 * WHY THE HOURS BOX OPENS ON --rule-strong AND THE ROWS CLOSE ON --line. The
 * design writes #17181A for the box's top rule and #C8C7C2 for each row's, so
 * they are two different marks and must stay two different tokens. --rule-strong
 * was chosen over --ink for the strong one because they are the same hex here
 * but not the same behaviour: buildOverrideCss emits `--ink: palette.ink` and
 * `--line: palette.line` (grepped, both present), and emits nothing for
 * --rule-strong (grepped, absent). Writing --ink would let an admin scheme move
 * this interior rule while the section rules .sec draws above and below it stay
 * put — one rule out of step with the two it is meant to echo.
 *
 * WHY THE PIN FLAG IS --ink / --on-ink AND NOT --ink / --paper. The design's flag
 * is #EFEEEA on #17181A, which both pairs describe natively. Only the first pair
 * is recomputed TOGETHER by the engine (--ink and --on-ink are both emitted;
 * --on-ink is derived against the scheme's ink), so only the first pair stays
 * legible if a scheme moves --ink.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `location` group, so each data-field below is a hook a real sidebar
 * input owns — a data-field on any other path is dead):
 *   location.headline                 — the h2, AND THE ADDRESS LINE. Emitted
 *                                       with set:html so an owner's own <em>
 *                                       picks up the design's second voice.
 *   location.sub                      — the arrival note under the headline
 *   location.rules.<original i>.label — the DAY cell of an hours row
 *   location.rules.<original i>.value — the TIME cell of an hours row
 *   location.hours                    — the closing mono line
 *
 * READ BUT NOT PRINTED (each already owns its own sidebar input; a second hook
 * here would be two inputs fighting over one value):
 *   location.address, falling back to layout.contact.address — feeds the
 *     client-side geocode, which is how this section finds its own pin
 *   location.lat / location.lng, falling back to layout.contact.lat / .lng —
 *     coordinates, not copy: they are numbers the sidebar edits as numbers, and
 *     a data-field would offer to typo them into the page
 *
 * WHY location.rules AND NOT location.hours FOR THE DAY/TIME ROWS. The schema
 * declares `location.hours` as a single kind:'text' field ("Hours line") and
 * `location.rules` as the kind:'list' of {label,value} pairs. The design's stack
 * is a REPEATING two-cell row, so it needs the list. Emitting
 * `location.hours.<i>.day` instead is not a style choice — it is the exact data
 * loss the schema's own comment at that field warns about: the deep writer would
 * convert the existing `location.hours` STRING into an array and take the
 * owner's hours line with it. So the rows are location.rules.*, and
 * location.hours keeps its own scalar slot beneath them, which is also where the
 * design puts its closing line.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed here, never invented, never an editable hook):
 *   · the rail label "08 / Location". It is an index of RENDER POSITION, not
 *     content: PageBN's order is Hero 00, About 01, Services 02, Why 03,
 *     Gallery 04, How 05, Testimonials 06, Credentials 07, LOCATION 08 — which
 *     is the number the design's own rail carries here. It is decorative,
 *     derived and never stored.
 *   · layout.businessName → the pin flag and the Leaflet marker's popup label.
 *     No placeholder: with no business name the flag simply leaves and the pin
 *     is a bare square, rather than a real street labelled "Your Business".
 *   · layout.contact.city → the map caption (the design's `shopTown`). Nothing
 *     derives it, so on a site without a city it is absent rather than guessed
 *     out of the address string.
 *   · rows drop blank entries but carry the ORIGINAL array index into data-field
 *     (R6), so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3. Nothing in
 *     this section is numbered on screen, so no display index is needed — the
 *     rail label is the section's index, not a row's.
 *   · a row survives on EITHER cell: a day the owner has typed but not yet
 *     answered is still their line. Only a wholly blank row goes, and it takes
 *     its hairline with it, so no rule is ever drawn under a row that is absent.
 *   · location.rules is read tolerantly (a bare string inside is treated as a
 *     label with no value). Draft content has arrived in both shapes before, and
 *     a `.map is not a function` costs the whole page rather than one row.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   · location.tag. The design's LOCATION draws NO eyebrow — the rail label is
 *     what names this section, and it is derived. Printing location.tag as a
 *     .kick would be adding a mark the handed-over design does not have.
 *   · location.phone. This section prints no number; the closing band and the
 *     footer own it. A tel: row here would be a control this design lacks (R2).
 *   · location.directions.text / .href. Schema-declared and used by siblings,
 *     but the design's LOCATION has no directions button. Adding one would be
 *     building a different section than the one that was handed over — and it is
 *     also why there is no button anywhere below, so R8's full-width-button pair
 *     (white-space: normal AND overflow-wrap: anywhere) has nothing to apply to.
 *   · NO SECOND ADDRESS PARAGRAPH. The design's info cell is h2 + note + rows +
 *     line, and its h2 IS the address line. location.address is not lost by
 *     being left out of the markup: it feeds the geocode below and FooterBN
 *     prints it, so it keeps a live sidebar input either way.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph / role="img" (R9). The map cell holds no upload — it is a
 *     DRAWING of a map, then real tiles — so it gets neither the wrapper's image
 *     frame nor an image role. The only accessible name available for it would
 *     be a sentence I wrote ("Map showing …"), which is exactly the
 *     component-supplied copy R2 and R9 forbid. The drawn face, its roads, its
 *     pin and its flag are therefore hidden from the tree by ONE aria-hidden, on
 *     the face and nowhere inside it — a literal aria-hidden beside a
 *     conditional one is the duplicated-attribute bug two earlier components
 *     shipped. Everything the drawing pictures is announced as real text beside
 *     it: the address headline, the caption and the hours rows. The rail carries
 *     the section's other aria-hidden, once, on the rail itself.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review.
 *     Stars belong to TESTIMONIALS and only ever drawn from a numeric rating;
 *     five stars for a customer who gave no number is a fabricated claim.
 *   · NO .reveal ON THE MAP CELL. Every other block here fades in. The map does
 *     not, because an opacity/transform transition running on the ancestor of a
 *     Leaflet container while that container is initialising is a needless
 *     interaction with a third-party widget, and the cell fades nothing the
 *     owner wrote — its one piece of owner text, the caption, is beside it.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER (R7), and this is a decision, not an
 *     omission. The helper exists to rewrite in-page hrefs onto this page's ids,
 *     and this section emits NO href at all — no button, no directions link, no
 *     phone number — so a copied helper would ship as dead code. The part of R7
 *     that DOES apply is honoured: id="visit", exactly as the design writes it
 *     and exactly the anchor PageBN's nav and closing CTA target. If a link is
 *     ever added here, copy the helper from a BN sibling with
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = menu · about · gallery · visit · faq · main and
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location,contact -> visit · services,rooms -> menu ·
 *     host,why -> about · work -> gallery · book -> main, passing tel: / mailto:
 *     / http through untouched.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). BODY content only: a real map (coordinates OR an address
 * behind it), an hours row, the closing line, or the arrival note. NOT the tag
 * and NOT the headline — derive-content-defaults.ts emits `location.tag: 'Visit'`
 * and `location.headline: 'Where to find us'` for EVERY site, so a gate counting
 * either would be always-true and would ship a heading over an empty ruled cell
 * on every first build. The two HALVES are gated again on their own, so a site
 * with only an arrival note keeps its paragraph and grows no empty 460px hole
 * beside it, and the survivor takes the full width AND loses the hairline —
 * because a rule down the middle of a half-empty sheet divides something from
 * nothing.
 *
 * .sec-body IS NOT USED, on purpose. It is the wrapper's PADDED content cell,
 * and this design's LOCATION cell has no padding of its own: the 48px 36px
 * belongs to the LEFT half only, so that the map can run full-bleed to the edge
 * of the sheet and the hours rules can run the full width of their half. Wearing
 * .sec-body and then cancelling its one declaration would name a primitive for
 * none of its effect. The cell keeps that primitive's other job — min-width: 0 —
 * as its own rule. .sec, .rail and .rail-t, the three that draw the SPINE, ARE
 * used exactly as the wrapper defines them and are rebuilt nowhere below.
 *
 * PRIMITIVES USED, AND THE ONE NOT USED. .h2 supplies the family, the 800, the
 * -.03em, the uppercase and the --ink; .rowrule supplies the flex row, the
 * baseline alignment and the --line hairline; .mono supplies the tracked 10px
 * caps in --muted. The arrival note takes NONE of them: .lede and .body both
 * paint --ink-soft, where this design's note is #3C3E42 = --ink-2, a full step
 * darker, and both clamp a size the design states flat — borrowing either would
 * be taking a primitive in order to disagree with four of its declarations. The
 * rust second voice inside the headline is PageBN's own `:where(.h2 em)` global
 * rule; a scoped rule could not reach markup made by set:html anyway (R3), and
 * this file therefore writes no :global() rule at all.
 *
 * TWO FORCED DEVIATIONS, both because the design draws a static picture where
 * ours can become a live tile map:
 *   1. The pin flag DROPS white-space: nowrap. The map cell clips overflow, so a
 *      long business name would be sliced off at both ends; wrapping beats
 *      truncating on the element that names the shop. The stack is CENTRED on
 *      the 44% crossing, so it is capped at twice the smaller side — 88% — less
 *      12px of air each side, and width: max-content is required alongside that
 *      or absolute shrink-to-fit collapses it to (containing block − left).
 *   2. The caption gains an OPAQUE --tint backing. In the design it sits on the
 *      drawn tint face; the moment Leaflet paints CARTO tiles underneath it,
 *      10px tracked caps over street labels are unreadable. The backing keeps
 *      the design's exact colour pair (--muted on --tint) rather than swapping
 *      in the wrapper's ink .cap pill, which would change the palette to solve a
 *      legibility problem. The padding insets the words ~10px/5px from the
 *      design's 16px corner; that is the whole cost.
 * No radius is added anywhere to compensate, because --r is 0 and a rounded
 * corner is the one thing this template cannot have.
 *
 * THE MAP, three tiers, the pattern the family shares:
 *   1. build-time lat/lng → window.__foodcraftBNInitMap(lat, lng, label)
 *   2. else any address   → client-side Nominatim lookup, then the same boot
 *   3. else               → the drawn plot-grid face, which IS the design
 * The drawn face lives INSIDE #map until Leaflet mounts; PageBN's boot runs
 * el.innerHTML = '' first, and that is what erases it. The literal id="map" and
 * the exact global name __foodcraftBNInitMap are a contract with PageBN — rename
 * either and the drawn face silently stays on screen underneath the tiles. The
 * caption is deliberately OUTSIDE #map: it is the owner's real text, not
 * fallback chrome, so it has to survive that erase and stay in the tree.
 *
 * A CLEARED coordinate field leaves '', which passes != null and which Number('')
 * turns into 0 — booting Leaflet and pinning the shop at 0,0 in the Gulf of
 * Guinea with its own name on the marker: a map asserting a location the owner
 * never gave. num() rejects blank and out-of-range values so a cleared field
 * falls through to the address lookup instead, and it uses ?? rather than || so
 * a genuine 0 survives.
 *
 * R8 · RESPONSIVE, and the clamp ladder TRACED rather than assumed — every
 * figure below is the arithmetic run out, not an estimate. The wrapper owns the
 * shared 760 and 520 pass; this file owns its own split, which collapses to one
 * column at 900px. Every TYPE size here is a single clamp declared ONCE at base
 * and touched by no media query in this file, so no floor in one query can sit
 * above a cap in another:
 *   headline  clamp(24px,3vw,40px)   → 40 (cap) at ≥1334px · 27 at 900 ·
 *                                      24 (floor) at ≤800px and every width below
 *   cell pad  clamp(26px,3.6vw,48px) → 48 (cap) at ≥1334px · 32 at 900 ·
 *                                      26 (floor) at ≤722px
 *             clamp(20px,2.8vw,36px) → 36 (cap) at ≥1286px · 25 at 900 ·
 *                                      20 (floor) at ≤714px
 * The ONE size a query moves is the map cell's min-height, and it moves DOWN,
 * once: the design's flat 460px holds for the whole two-column state, and the
 * 900 collapse hands it to clamp(240px,32vw,460px) — 288 at 900, 243 at 760,
 * 240 (floor) at ≤750px. 460 → 288 across that boundary, so the ladder is still
 * monotonic. An earlier draft put that clamp at BASE, which quietly shrank the
 * design's 460px cell to 410px at 1280 and 288px at 901 — inside the widths the
 * design actually specifies.
 * The headline's scoped rule — .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-h, (0,4,0) once Astro has
 * scoped both halves — out-specifies the wrapper's html[data-page] :where(.h2)
 * at (0,1,1), so it wins ALL FIVE of its declarations, the shared 760 pass
 * included. That is why max-width: none and overflow-wrap: anywhere are RESTATED
 * on it rather than left to that pass: left to it, they would not have landed.
 * Confining the clamp to a min-width query instead is what WOULD have produced
 * an inversion — the shared pass is clamp(26px,7.4vw,40px), capped at 40 from
 * 541px up, so the headline would have JUMPED from 24px to 40px crossing
 * 761px → 760px. Nothing else changes size in a query below; only tracking,
 * padding and the row's direction. At 360px nothing pushes sideways: every grid
 * track is minmax(0,…), every cell is min-width: 0, and every owner string
 * breaks anywhere.
 *
 * VERIFIED CONTRAST — COMPUTED for this section's own pairs (sRGB relative
 * luminance, WCAG 2.x, against PageBN's declared hexes), not estimated:
 *   ink/paper       15.30  the headline and the time cell
 *   ink-2/paper      9.23  the arrival note
 *   muted/paper      5.77  the day cell and the closing line
 *   muted/tint       5.17  the map caption, on its opaque backing
 *   accent/paper     5.67  the rail label
 *   on-ink/ink      15.30  the pin flag (--on-ink and --paper share a hex)
 * Nothing in this section is below AA; the tightest pair is 5.17.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;

const lay = layout || {};

const loc = content.location || {};
// Nothing beyond trim(): the headline goes out through set:html so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches PageBN's rust <em> rule.
const headline = typeof loc.headline === 'string' ? loc.headline.trim() : '';
const sub = typeof loc.sub === 'string' ? loc.sub.trim() : '';
// The schema's single "Hours line" text field — the design's closing mono line.
const hoursNote = typeof loc.hours === 'string' ? loc.hours.trim() : '';

// READ, NOT PRINTED. The design's info cell is headline + note + rows + line and
// its h2 IS the address; there is no second address paragraph. This value feeds
// tier 2 of the map below, and FooterBN prints it.
const address = String(loc.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();

// A row the owner added but never filled would spend a hairline and 24px of
// padding saying nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank), carry
// the ORIGINAL index (R6) — and note this filter is what makes the gate honest:
// an array of nothing but blank rows no longer keeps the section alive.
type HourRow = { i: number; day: string; time: string };
const rows: HourRow[] = (Array.isArray(loc.rules) ? loc.rules : [])
  .map((row: any, i: number) => {
    // An item may legitimately be {label,value} (what the schema's newItem
    // creates) or, from an older draft, a bare string — treated as a label with
    // no value rather than dropped.
    const item = row && typeof row === 'object' ? row : { label: row, value: '' };
    return {
      i,
      day: String(item.label ?? '').trim(),
      time: String(item.value ?? '').trim(),
    };
  })
  .filter((r: HourRow) => Boolean(r.day || r.time));

const num = (v: any, max: number): number | null => {
  if (v === null || v === undefined || String(v).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(v);
  return Number.isFinite(n) && Math.abs(n) <= max ? n : null;
};
const lat = num(loc.lat, 90) ?? num(lay.contact?.lat, 90);
const lng = num(loc.lng, 180) ?? num(lay.contact?.lng, 180);
const hasCoords = lat !== null && lng !== null;
const hasAddress = Boolean(address);
// The drawn face is a picture claiming where this shop physically is. With
// neither coordinates nor an address there is nothing real behind that claim, so
// the whole cell leaves and the info half takes the full width.
const hasMap = hasCoords || hasAddress;

const brand = String(lay.businessName ?? '').trim();
const locality = String(lay.contact?.city ?? '').trim();

// The INFO half may keep a bare headline once the section is alive for another
// reason — a headline over a real map is not a heading over nothing.
const hasInfo = Boolean(headline || sub || hoursNote) || rows.length > 0;
// R5 — BODY content only. Headline and tag are excluded: both are derived onto
// every site, so counting either would make this gate always-true.
const hasSection = Boolean(sub || hoursNote) || rows.length > 0 || hasMap;
const solo = !(hasInfo && hasMap);
---
{hasSection && (
  <section class="sec pcw-vis" id="visit">
    {/* THE SPINE. .sec, .rail and .rail-t are PageBN's primitives, used as they
        are: the 52px track, the right hairline and the rotated mono index. The
        label is decorative and derived, so the whole rail is hidden from the
        accessibility tree — once, here, and nowhere inside it. */}
    <div class="rail" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="rail-t">Location</span>
    </div>
    <div class:list={['pcw-vis-body', solo && 'pcw-vis-body--solo']}>
      {hasInfo && (
        <div class="pcw-vis-info">
          {headline && (
            <h2 class="h2 pcw-vis-h reveal" data-field="location.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
          )}
          {/* location.sub is a plain textarea in the schema, so it is bound as
              TEXT. The headline above is the one place markup is invited. */}
          {sub && (
            <p class="pcw-vis-sub reveal" data-delay="1" data-field="location.sub">{sub}</p>
          )}
          {rows.length > 0 && (
            <div class="pcw-vis-hours reveal" data-delay="2">
              {rows.map((r) => (
                /* Each cell is guarded on its own, so a half-filled row prints
                   one cell rather than one cell plus an empty span. The row
                   itself was already dropped above if BOTH were blank, which is
                   what keeps the hairline honest. */
                <div class="rowrule pcw-vis-row">
                  {r.day && (
                    <span class="mono pcw-vis-d" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.label`}>{r.day}</span>
                  )}
                  {r.time && (
                    <span class="pcw-vis-t" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.value`}>{r.time}</span>
                  )}
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
          {hoursNote && (
            <p class="mono pcw-vis-note reveal" data-delay="3" data-field="location.hours">{hoursNote}</p>
          )}
        </div>
      )}
      {hasMap && (
        <div class="pcw-vis-map">
          <div id="map">
            {/* Tier-3 only, and entirely decorative: PageBN's boot empties #map
                before Leaflet draws, so tiles never stack on top of this. ONE
                aria-hidden, here and nowhere else in the subtree. */}
            <div class="pcw-vis-face" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="pcw-vis-road pcw-vis-road-h"></span>
              <span class="pcw-vis-road pcw-vis-road-v"></span>
              <span class="pcw-vis-pin">
                <span class="pcw-vis-dot"></span>
                {brand && <span class="pcw-vis-flag">{brand}</span>}
              </span>
            </div>
          </div>
          {/* OUTSIDE #map on purpose: the city is the owner's real text, not
              fallback chrome, so it has to survive the boot that erases the face
              — and it stays in the accessibility tree. */}
          {locality && <span class="mono pcw-vis-cap">{locality}</span>}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
{hasCoords && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lat, lng, brand }}>
    (function () {
      // Bounded on purpose: this script is emitted where the section sits, ABOVE
      // the wrapper's boot, so one retry is the normal case — but an unbounded
      // loop would spin a timer forever on any page where that boot never ran.
      var attempt = 0;
      (function init() {
        if (window.__foodcraftBNInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBNInitMap(lat, lng, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(init, 80); }
      })();
    })();
  </script>
)}
{!hasCoords && hasAddress && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ address, brand }}>
    (function geocodeThenInit() {
      var attempt = 0;
      function tryInit(la, ln) {
        if (window.__foodcraftBNInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBNInitMap(la, ln, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(function () { tryInit(la, ln); }, 80); }
      }
      try {
        var query = encodeURIComponent(String(address).replace(/\n/g, ', '));
        var url = 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&limit=1&q=' + query;
        fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'en' } })
          .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : []; })
          .then(function (results) {
            if (!Array.isArray(results) || results.length === 0) return;
            var la = parseFloat(results[0].lat);
            var ln = parseFloat(results[0].lon);
            if (isNaN(la) || isNaN(ln)) return;
            tryInit(la, ln);
          })
          .catch(function () { /* keep the drawn face: it is the design */ });
      } catch (e) { /* never block render */ }
    })();
  </script>
)}
<style>
  /* THE CONTENT CELL IS UNPADDED ON PURPOSE — see the frontmatter. The padding
     in this section belongs to the LEFT half, so the map can run full-bleed to
     the edge of the sheet. min-width:0 because it is a grid child of .sec and it
     must be allowed to shrink rather than widen the document. */
  .pcw-vis-body {
    min-width: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  /* One half survives its own guard: the other track goes rather than standing
     empty at 460px. */
  .pcw-vis-body--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* THE INFO HALF. The design writes `padding:48px 36px`; those two numbers are
     the CAPS of the clamps below, so a desktop renders the design's value and
     the only movement is downward as the viewport narrows (R8). The border-right
     is the design's interior hairline — the quiet --line, never the
     --rule-strong that .sec draws to close the whole section. */
  .pcw-vis-info {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: clamp(26px, 3.6vw, 48px) clamp(20px, 2.8vw, 36px);
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* A half-empty sheet gets no rule down its middle: with no map there is
     nothing on the other side of it to divide from. */
  .pcw-vis-body--solo .pcw-vis-info { border-right: 0; }

  /* THE HEADLINE, which is the ADDRESS. .h2 supplies the family, the 800, the
     -.03em, the uppercase and the --ink; the four metrics this section states
     differently are set here. max-width is released rather than inherited: .h2
     caps at 20ch and the design writes no cap at all, and this heading already
     sits in a half-width cell — 20ch would break a street line two words earlier
     than the design does. Declared ONCE, with no query of its own, which is what
     keeps the ladder monotonic across 760 and 520 (traced in the frontmatter). */
  .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-h {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 3vw, 40px);
    line-height: 1.04;
    margin: 0 0 .3em;
    max-width: none;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* THE ARRIVAL NOTE. Deliberately NOT .lede and NOT .body: both paint
     --ink-soft, and this design's note is the darker --ink-2; both clamp a size
     the design states flat at 15px. Borrowing either would mean overriding four
     of its declarations. The parent-class qualifier puts this rule above
     PageBN's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}`, which is (0,1,1), whatever order the
     sheets land in. */
  .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-sub {
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: 40ch;
    margin: 0 0 24px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The design's 24px is the gap to the hours box. With no rows and no closing
     line it would be dead space above the cell's own bottom padding. */
  .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-sub:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  /* THE HOURS BOX opens on the FULL-STRENGTH rule, which is the design's own
     distinction: the ink weight above the stack, the grey hairline under each
     row. --rule-strong rather than --ink so this interior rule cannot drift away
     from the section rules it echoes — see the frontmatter for the grep behind
     that. */
  .pcw-vis-hours { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); }

  /* .rowrule brings the flex row, the baseline alignment and the --line hairline
     under every row INCLUDING the last — that closing rule above the cell's
     bottom padding is the design, which is why there is no ":not(:last-child)"
     trick here. Three things are restated: the design pushes the cells apart,
     opens the gutter to 16px, and uses 12px of vertical padding where the
     primitive uses 13px.
     A FOURTH difference is deliberately NOT restated. This row's inline style
     writes no align-items, so the design renders it at the flex default of
     stretch — which top-aligns a 10.5px mono day against a 14.5px time and
     leaves their baselines about 3px apart. .rowrule's baseline is kept
     instead, because the design's OWN other label/value row (the ABOUT spec
     grid) writes align-items:baseline for exactly this pairing, and because
     this row is a shared primitive three sections are meant to draw alike. */
  .pcw-vis-row {
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 12px 0;
  }
  /* The DAY cell is .mono — the tracked caps in --muted — with the three
     properties the design sets differently: 10.5px rather than 10px, .12em of
     tracking rather than .2em, and the body's regular weight rather than .mono's
     500. line-height: normal because the design sets no leading on this span and
     `normal` is literally what it draws; PageBN's body 1.62 otherwise leaks in
     and inflates the drawn ~43px row toward ~48px. `normal` rather than a
     number, because guessing a number would be a divergence of its own. */
  .pcw-vis-row .pcw-vis-d {
    font-size: 10.5px;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: .12em;
    line-height: normal;
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The TIME cell: the design's 600 at 14.5px in --ink, in the sans it inherits
     from the body. NOT the wrapper's .fig — that primitive paints --accent, and
     an opening time here is drawn in ink. `margin-left:auto` and
     `text-align:right` are the two properties the design does not write, and
     both are no-ops in the state it draws: space-between already puts a two-cell
     row's value hard against the right edge. They take effect only when the DAY
     is missing (one child, which space-between would leave at the left) and when
     a long time WRAPS, where a ragged-left second line under a right-aligned
     first is not what the design's row looks like. */
  .pcw-vis-row .pcw-vis-t {
    margin-left: auto;
    text-align: right;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: normal;
    color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* THE CLOSING LINE is .mono again — same family, same 10px, same caps, same
     --muted — restating only the design's tighter .1em, its regular weight, its
     generous 1.9 leading and its 16px lead-in. */
  .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-note {
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    line-height: 1.9;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* An owner with a closing line and no rows is a real case, and then the
     design's 16px lead-in would push it off the cell's own top padding. */
  .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-note:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

  /* THE MAP HALF. Not the wrapper's .plate: that frame is for an UPLOAD and
     carries its own --well ground and min-height, where the design draws this
     cell flat on --tint, full-bleed, with no border and no corner of its own.
     min-height is the design's own flat 460px and stays flat for the whole of
     the two-column state; it is the 900 query below — where the cell stops
     being a half and becomes the full sheet — that puts it on a curve.
     overflow:hidden is the design's, and it is also what clips a live tile map
     to the cell. */
  .pcw-vis-map {
    position: relative;
    min-width: 0;
    min-height: 460px;
    background-color: var(--tint);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  /* The literal id is a contract with PageBN's boot — see the frontmatter. */
  #map { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: 0; }
  /* The plotted face: a 40px ink grid, drawn from --ink-rgb so an admin scheme
     moves the plot lines with the ink everywhere else on the sheet. No
     background-color of its own — the cell's --tint is the ground, exactly as
     the design layers it. */
  .pcw-vis-face {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, rgba(var(--ink-rgb), .09) 0 1px, transparent 1px 40px),
      repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(var(--ink-rgb), .09) 0 1px, transparent 1px 40px);
  }
  .pcw-vis-road { position: absolute; background-color: rgba(var(--accent-rgb), .35); }
  .pcw-vis-road-h { top: 48%; left: 0; right: 0; height: 5px; }
  .pcw-vis-road-v { top: 0; bottom: 0; left: 44%; width: 4px; }
  /* The cap sits on the ABSPOS pin, not on the flag. The stack is CENTRED on the
     44% crossing, so it only stays inside the cell when it is capped at twice
     the smaller side — 88% — less 12px of air each side. calc(100% - 24px) would
     still let a long shop name run past the left edge and be clipped there by
     overflow:hidden, which is the truncation the wrapping flag exists to avoid.
     width: max-content is required alongside it, or absolute shrink-to-fit
     collapses the stack to (containing block − left). */
  .pcw-vis-pin {
    position: absolute;
    top: 48%;
    left: 44%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -100%);
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    width: max-content;
    max-width: calc(88% - 24px);
    text-align: center;
    z-index: 1;
  }
  /* SQUARE, because nothing in this design is round: no border-radius is set
     here and none is inherited. 13px is the border-box, which is what the
     design's own `*{box-sizing:border-box}` gives it too. */
  .pcw-vis-dot {
    width: 13px;
    height: 13px;
    background-color: var(--accent);
    border: 3px solid var(--paper);
  }
  /* Ink flag, paper label — the design's own pair. line-height: normal and
     weight 400 because the design states neither and both are what it draws;
     nowrap is dropped and replaced by the wrap pair, explained in the
     frontmatter. */
  .pcw-vis-flag {
    background-color: var(--ink);
    color: var(--on-ink);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: .14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: normal;
    padding: 5px 10px;
    text-align: center;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* .mono supplies the design's 10px caps in --muted. Local: the design's .16em
     tracking, its regular weight, `line-height: normal` because it sets no
     leading, and the opaque backing that keeps this legible once real tiles are
     underneath it — the design's own colour pair, not the wrapper's ink .cap
     pill, so the palette is unchanged. */
  .pcw-vis-map .pcw-vis-cap {
    position: absolute;
    left: 16px;
    bottom: 16px;
    z-index: 2;
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: .16em;
    line-height: normal;
    background-color: var(--tint);
    padding: 5px 10px;
    max-width: calc(100% - 32px);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* R8 · ONE COLUMN BEFORE EITHER HALF IS SQUEEZED. At 900px each half is under
     425px: the address headline starts breaking mid-street and the map is too
     narrow to place a pin usefully. The info half's rule turns from a right
     hairline into a bottom one, so the sheet stays ruled in one column, and it
     stays absent when there is only one half. Nothing here changes a font-size —
     the three clamps above are declared once at base and are not touched by this
     query or the next, so no floor here can sit above a cap there. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .pcw-vis-body { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pcw-vis-info {
      border-right: 0;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .pcw-vis-body--solo .pcw-vis-info { border-bottom: 0; }
    /* Released: once the cell is the full sheet, a 40ch cap strands the note in
       a wide empty gutter. */
    .pcw-vis-info .pcw-vis-sub { max-width: none; }
    /* The design's 460px is a TWO-COLUMN figure — the height of a cell sitting
       BESIDE the hours — so it is kept verbatim for exactly as long as that is
       what the cell is. Stacked, the same 460px is half a phone screen of empty
       tint above the fold, so the height goes to a curve HERE and nowhere else:
       288 at 900, 243 at 760, 240 (floor) at ≤750. 460 → 288 across this
       boundary, so it only ever falls. The cap is inert inside a max-width
       query, where 32vw can never exceed 288px; it is written so the
       declaration names the value it came down from. AboutBN's plate and
       HeroBN's band are stepped the same way. */
    .pcw-vis-map { min-height: clamp(240px, 32vw, 460px); }
  }
  /* 520 · nothing may push the page sideways at 360px. The rail has already
     collapsed by here (PageBN's shared 760 pass). At 360px the info cell is 320px
     wide inside its 20px padding floors, and a full day label beside a full
     opening range is more than that holds — the time wraps to a ragged second
     line under a right-aligned first. The row stacks instead: day above, time
     below, both left. Only tracking and position move — no size is touched. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .pcw-vis-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 4px;
    }
    .pcw-vis-row .pcw-vis-t { margin-left: 0; text-align: left; }
    .pcw-vis-flag { letter-spacing: .12em; padding: 5px 8px; }
    .pcw-vis-map .pcw-vis-cap {
      left: 12px;
      bottom: 12px;
      letter-spacing: .14em;
      max-width: calc(100% - 24px);
    }
  }
</style>
